How to Use the Project
1. Clone the Repository
get from Main.py
2. Install Required Libraries

Install the required Python libraries:

pip install torch torchvision numpy matplotlib pillow opencv-python tqdm

If you are using Google Colab, most of these libraries may already be installed.

3. Prepare the Dataset

Download the datasets from the links above and organize the image and mask files into the correct folders.

Each image must have a matching mask. For example:

train/images/000001.jpg
train/masks/000001.png

Make sure the image and mask filenames match. If there are more images than masks, the training code may give an error.

4. Train the Model

Run the training script:

python train.py

The model will train on the food segmentation dataset and save the trained weights.

5. Evaluate the Model

After training, run:

python evaluate.py

This will test the model and show results such as segmentation accuracy and Dice Score.

6. Test With Your Own Food Image

You can upload or place a food image inside the project folder and run the prediction code.

Example:

python main.py

The program will:

Load the food image
Predict the food segmentation mask
Display the original image and predicted mask
Estimate basic nutrition information
Notes

The dataset files are not included in this GitHub repository because they are large. Please download them manually from the official dataset links.

Make sure the dataset folder paths match the code. If the program says that images or masks are missing, check that your folders are named correctly:

images
masks
train
val
test

Also make sure every image has a matching mask file.

Project Goal

The goal of this project is to create a simple Food AI system that can segment food from an image and use that result to support nutrition estimation. Accurate segmentation is important because the nutrition estimation depends on how well the model separates the food area from the background.
